# 2026-05-13 18:47:39.350958
+He analizado el repositorio sowmh/Lum-OS y, efectivamente, el sistema actual es un kernel monolítico básico en modo texto VGA (80x25).Aquí tienes el prompt depurado. He eliminado las menciones a la configuración de la GDT, IDT, paginación básica y el shell de texto, ya que ya están implementados en el repositorio. Este prompt se enfoca exclusivamente en la transición técnica de texto a píxeles.Technical Specification: Lum-OS Graphical Evolution (Updated)Objective: Evolve Lum-OS from its current VGA Text Mode (0xB8000) to a VESA VBE 2.0 LFB system ($1024 \times 768 \times 32$bpp) with a custom compositor and window manager.1. Analysis of Existing BaseThe repository already provides:Memory Management: Physical bitmap and virtual paging (PDE/PTE).Interrupts: IDT and PIC remapping (IRQ 0-15).Input: Keyboard driver (IRQ 1) and basic Shell logic.Filesystem: FAT12 implementation for reading files.Action: Integrate the new GUI layer without breaking the existing memory allocation and IRQ handling.Phase 1: VESA VBE 2.0 Initialization (Stage 2)Currently, stage2.asm jumps to Protected Mode immediately. You must modify this to negotiate graphics:1.1 Graphics Mode SetupIn src/bootloader/stage2.asm, before enabling the cr0 protection bit:Detect VBE: Use int 0x10, ax=0x4F00.Set Mode: Target mode 0x118 ($1024 \times 768 \times 32$ bits, Linear Framebuffer).Information Transfer: Capture the ModeInfoBlock (specifically the physical address of the Framebuffer) and pass it to the kernel via the existing boot_info structure.Phase 2: Framebuffer Mapping & GFX Engine2.1 Virtual MappingIn the kernel's memory initialization:Map the physical LFB address (from VESA) to a fixed virtual address (e.g., 0xFD000000).Optimization: Use the Page Attribute Table (PAT) or set PCD/PWT bits in the page tables to enable Write-Combining, which is essential for GUI performance.2.2 Software Renderer (gfx.c)Since Lum-OS lacks a C standard library, implement:gfx_put_pixel: Write directly to the mapped uint32_t* buffer.gfx_fill_rect: Use optimized loops for clearing regions.gfx_draw_line & gfx_draw_circle: Pure integer-based algorithms (Bresenham/Midpoint).Alpha Blending: Implement a function to blend two 0xAARRGGBB colors for window shadows and transparencies.Phase 3: Input & Assets3.1 PS/2 Mouse Driver (mouse.c)Initialize the auxiliary PS/2 port (Command 0xA8).Implement a handler for IRQ 12.Process the 3-byte movement packets to update a global mouse_x and mouse_y.Cursor Rendering: Implement a 12x20 bitmap cursor that restores the background to avoid flickering.3.2 Font Rendering (font.c)Replace vga_print with font_print.Embed a PSF1 bitmap font (8x16) to render text on the framebuffer.Phase 4: Window Manager & Compositor (wm.c)Transform the existing CLI into a windowed application:Z-Order Management: A linked list of windows where the last element is the "top" window.Dirty Rectangles: Instead of redrawing the whole screen (3MB per frame), only redraw areas where the mouse moves or a window updates.Window Decorations:Title bars with "Close", "Min", and "Max" buttons.Active/Inactive borders (Blue #58A6FF vs Gray #30363D).Existing Shell Integration: Wrap the current shell.c logic into a Window structure. Instead of writing to 0xB8000, the shell will now write to a back-buffer assigned to its window.Phase 5: New System ApplicationsImplement the following modules as graphical windows:SysMonitor: Uses the current PIT and Memory info to draw real-time usage graphs.FileBrowser: A graphical interface for the FAT12 driver already in the repo.Taskbar: A permanent bottom strip showing the "Start" menu and clock.ConstraintsDo Not Modify: The GDT setup or the Stage 1 MBR logic.Memory: Ensure the Framebuffer mapping does not overlap with the Kernel Heap or the Stack Guard Page.Build System: Update the Makefile to include the new .c files in the final KERNEL.BIN.Next Step: Provide the summary of how the current vga.c and stage2.asm interact before starting the VESA implementation.

